From 9e91657056a0b009b27e36e3eb03d5fba88d8e55 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 18:45:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Pin the npm that runs the publish instead of floating it

`npm install -g npm@latest` resolves to whatever npm published most
recently, on a job that holds the OIDC credential and uploads the
tarball. Today that is npm 12, a major the release path has never been
run on; tomorrow it is whatever lands next. The upgrade is not even
needed for the stated reason: setup-node pins node-version 24.x, whose
bundled npm is 11.19.0, and trusted publishing requires 11.5.1 or
later.

`^11.5.1` keeps the floor the step exists to state, resolves inside the
major the release has been run on, and cannot cross into a new one on
release day. The concrete failure this avoids is not hypothetical:
npm 12's engines are `^22.22.2 || ^24.15.0 || >=26.0.0`, so a later
major raising its Node floor past whatever `24.x` resolves to would
make the upgrade step itself fail and block the release.

Remove patch/npm-pin.patch and its row in patch/README.md in the
commit that applies this.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W4X2MprZjtGPiBVth8jTrW
---
 .github/workflows/publish.yml | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/.github/workflows/publish.yml b/.github/workflows/publish.yml
index 66a5b22..d5ed08a 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/publish.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/publish.yml
@@ -103,7 +103,7 @@ jobs:
       - run: npm run test-cov-check
 
       - name: Use a trusted-publishing capable npm
-        run: npm install -g npm@latest
+        run: npm install -g 'npm@^11.5.1'
 
       - name: Is this version already on npm?
         id: registry
